{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "target_locations",
      "description": "During button pressing and retraction, requested covers exchanged positions and sometimes continued moving after the hand reached its waypoint. The policy generally chose the correct button waypoint, but its stored target positions sometimes followed the wrong exchange branch or differed from the annotation by more than 1 cm. Some annotations also required an elevation estimate that the available images did not establish. This breaks PREDICTABILITY. Retain the ordered list of requested targets' central-axis world [x,y] coordinates, but use a reproducible geometric landmark: reconstruct the entire horizontal top square from its opposing outer edges and use its diagonal intersection, not the center of a bright patch, inset panel, visible side, or silhouette. Use approximately z=0.06 m only for a resting cover. For a cover elevated during an exchange, require visible geometric evidence for its elevation, such as a resolvable known-size top square or simultaneous calibrated views; otherwise retain the last reliably localized coordinate until localization becomes possible. Do not fill an intermediate coordinate using later frames or an unobservable assumed height. Recheck existing intermediate annotations under this convention rather than treating their millimetre-rounded numbers as ground truth. Follow identity through every available intermediate frame, including changes in image ranking; update after residual translation even during an unrelated button approach. Preserve coordinates only when the images support stationarity. An example ordered value is [[0.107, -0.009], [-0.027, 0.152]]. Keep the existing world-coordinate comparison tolerance.",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "target_locations",
      "description": "During exchanges triggered while the hand was pressing or retracting from buttons, the button action was usually correct but the target memory could follow the wrong cover or localize the right cover from an inconsistent landmark. This breaks PREDICTABILITY, not a demonstrated need for more hidden-color fields. Keep the fixed requested-order list of central-axis [x,y] coordinates. Establish correspondence from the incoming locations and retained frame, then trace each target through every chronological frame before computing its endpoint coordinate. In particular, continue a moving cover's trajectory when it passes below another cover; do not reconnect it to the cover occupying its former upper or lower slot. Reconstruct the full horizontal top square using opposing outer edges and their corners; its center is the intersection of the diagonals. Do not use a bright patch, inset panel, side face, or overall body center. Project that center at approximately z=0.06 m only when the cover is resting. During an airborne exchange, estimate elevation only if the known-size face geometry or available calibrated views actually resolve it; if not, carry the last reliable coordinate rather than projecting onto the resting plane or guessing a correction. Update once reliable geometry becomes visible again. Compare successive front frames for residual translation: reaching a button waypoint does not imply that covers have settled. Preserve a coordinate only when the observed cover is stationary, not merely because its identity is unchanged. For example, a red target may finish at [0.107, -0.009] while the green target continues moving. Write the resulting list to millimetre resolution without claiming that measurement accuracy."
    },
    {
      "name": "tracking_frame",
      "description": "After the first of two requested pickups had been released and the empty hand withdrawn, the policy correctly approached the remaining target but retained an older tracking frame because other covers were occluded. The annotation advanced to the current frame. This is a PREDICTABILITY failure in applying the existing still-needed qualification. Explicitly define still-needed targets as the requested entries at indices greater than or equal to the observed updated pickups_completed count. Use that updated count when evaluating frame eligibility in the same cycle. Already successfully lifted targets do not need to be visible, even if they have been covered again. Write the latest shown frame in which every remaining target has unambiguous identity and localization from the views actually available for that frame. Retain the previous label if none qualifies. For example, after the first pickup, a current label such as E380 qualifies when the second target alone is clearly localized, regardless of occlusion of the first target or distractors. This clarifies the existing annotator prompt rather than changing which frames it should select."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "Do not add episode-specific pixel paths, final coordinates, or color-to-slot shortcuts. The reported wrong exchange branch is addressed by chronological correspondence, not by memorizing a particular red-cover path. Do not invent an exact top height or an elevation correction solely to reproduce annotated numbers: the supplied evidence does not establish such a constant, and future-only reconstruction would make the runtime memory unpredictable. Instead, apply the same observable landmark and reliability convention to annotation and policy. Do not widen the 1 cm coordinate tolerance to hide these discrepancies. The reports about already-completed targets being irrelevant to tracking-frame eligibility and about residual motion do not justify new memory fields; they clarify existing rules. Failures that supplied no missing-information account do not establish an additional action-rule defect. The button waypoints were generally consistent with the intended milestone, and the supplied evidence does not justify replacing the button localization or interaction-state rules. No action-instruction change is proposed."
}

